{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":19,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"model_calls":19,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":19,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1175329779#3_2438380177","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q149","rank":6,"snippet":"<cover>Mexican muralism started a tradition that continues to this day in Mexico. In 1921, after the end of the Mexican Revolution, José Vasconcelos was appointed to head the Secretaría de Educación Pública. At the time, most of the Mexican population was illiterate and the government needed a way to promote the ideals of the Mexican Revolution. Vasconcelos helped establish a government-backed mural program, hiring the country’s best artists, for this purpose. Similar to mural use in the pre-Hispanic and colonial periods, the purpose of these murals were not simply aesthetic, but social, to promote certain ideals. These ideals or principles were to glorify the Mexican Revolution and the identity of Mexico as a mestizo nation, with the indigenous promoted as well as the Spanish. The movement was strongest from the 1920s to the 1950s, which corresponded to the country’s transformation from a mostly rural and mostly illiterate society to an industrialized one. While today they are part of Mexico’s identity, at the time they were controversial, especially those with socialist messages plastered on centuries-old colonial buildings.\" , \"start_char\": 2191.000000 , \"title\": \"The </cover>"},"time":16},"model_calls":19,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":13,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
