{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":24,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q150"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q150"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1342511605#1_2485324887","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q150","rank":5,"snippet":"Influence and Legacy Follow These Artists Follow These Galleries \" , \"segment\": \"indeed, it is probably safe to say that no other movement proposed and produced art for the people quite like the Mexican mural painting, intertwined with its nation and tradition with such dedication and vision. Diego Rivera - La historia de México- de la conquista al futuro, 1929-1935. Mural at Palacio Nacional, Ciudad de México<cover>Mexican Muralism - Hand-in-Hand with Politics and People After the Mexican Revolution, which had dictator Porfirio Díaz and his regime overthrown, and the political turmoil that followed for the next few years, Mexico saw the creation of a new party, the Partido Revolucionario Institucional - the PRI. The land was finally in the hands of its own workers, </cover>but there was so much to be done: the new government sought to establish a brand new Mexican society, founded on its rich tradition but very much forward-looking. Since most of the population was illiterate, an effective way to change that would be through the creation of visual messages that would not only appeal aesthetically, but that would also promote certain ideals. And so, Mexican muralism became a vital part ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1342511605#1_2485324887","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":24,"document_judgments":21,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
