{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":29,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q153"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q153"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_135778196#13_149884004","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q153","rank":2,"snippet":"Representation in other media Gallery Paintings Murals Sculptures See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"For then man will have thrown off all of his superstitions and irrational taboos.\\\" In his later years Rivera lived in the United States and Mexico. He died on November 24, 1957. Career in Mexico In 1920, urged by Alberto J. Pani, the Mexican ambassador to France, Rivera left France and traveled through Italy studying its art, including Renaissance frescoes. After José Vasconcelos became Minister of <cover>Education, Rivera returned to Mexico in 1921 to become involved in the government sponsored Mexican mural program planned by Vasconcelos. See also Mexican muralism. </cover>The program included such Mexican artists as José Clemente Orozco, David Alfaro Siqueiros, and Rufino Tamayo, and the French artist Jean Charlot. In January 1922, he painted – experimentally in encaustic – his first significant mural Creation in the Bolívar Auditorium of the National Preparatory School in Mexico City while guarding himself with a pistol against right-wing students. In the autumn of 1922, Rivera participated in the founding of the Revolutionary Union of Technical Workers, Painters and Sculptors, and later that year he joined the Mexican Communist Party (including "},"time":16},"model_calls":29,"document_judgments":24,"relevance_3":18,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
