{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":53,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q164"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q164"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_433912029#7_872375920","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q164","rank":6,"snippet":", \"end_char\": 6210.000000 , \"headings\": \"El sueño (La noche de los pobres)/Sleep (The night of the poor) The Art El sueño (La noche de los pobres)/Sleep (The night of the poor) The Artist Key Ideas Discussion Questions \" , \"segment\": \"In 1921, Rivera returned to Mexico for the first time since his departure in 1907. The post-revolutionary Mexican government was enacting a series of reforms to promote and solidify its nationalist and socialist <cover>ideals, and Vasconcelos recognized the power of murals for this purpose. He developed a government-sponsored public </cover>art program that commissioned artists to paint murals in government buildings throughout Mexico. Diego Rivera, David Alfaro Siqueiros, and José Clemente Orozco were among the most prominent of these artists and, together, became known as Los tres grandes (The Three Great Ones) for their contributions to Mexican art during this period. The public mural program promoted what was known as Mexican Modernism; centered in Mexico City during the 1920s and 1930s, artists and intellectuals contributed to this dynamic period of artistic achievement through writing, painting, drawing, and photography. Though much of Rivera’"},"time":9},"model_calls":53,"document_judgments":45,"relevance_3":28,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
