{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":56,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q165"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q165"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1196967126#8_2099887362","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q165","rank":5,"snippet":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1196967126#8_2099887362\" , \"end_char\": 7049.000000 , \"headings\": \"Social Realism Social Realism Started: 1929 Social Realism \\\"To me propaganda is a holy word.\\\" Summary of Social Realism Key Ideas & Accomplishments Overview of Social Realism Key Artists Do Not Miss Important <cover>Art and Artists of Social Realism Cover image for the New Masses (1933) Aspects of Negro Life: Song of the Towers (1934) Death (Lynched Figure) (1934) Useful Resources on Social Realism Books Share Do more \" , \"segment\": \"Do Not Miss Harlem Renaissance Quick view Read more The Harlem Renaissance was the name given to the cultural, social, and artistic explosion that took place in Harlem between the end of World War I and the middle of the 1930s. Mexican Muralism</cover>Quick view Read more Mexican Muralism was a vibrant movement whose artists created large-scale works, executed in Mexico and the US. Relying on a mixture of Mexican heritage and classical Italian styles, murals were frequently done in a figurative, allegorical style and dealt with political, social, and radical themes. Socialist Realism Quick view Read more Socialist Realism ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_09_1196967126#8_2099887362","rank":5,"relevance":2}]},"time":8},"model_calls":56,"document_judgments":47,"relevance_3":30,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":56,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q165"},"model_calls":56,"document_judgments":47,"relevance_3":30,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-36155","model_call":56,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q165"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q165"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_1522095625#2_2697536764","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q165","rank":6,"snippet":"1522095625#2_2697536764\" , \"end_char\": 1848.000000 , \"headings\": \" \" , \"segment\": \"The English name of the painting is \\\"the one who eats\\\", and the artist chose it as a response to one of the leaders of modern art, who claimed that Brazilians would gobble up European culture. Diego Rivera was married to ______________. Frida Kahlo What do the strong black outlined images in the painting above represent? The stained glass windows seen in many Cuban homes. How did <cover>Mexican murals affect influence the social structure? They brought art to all social groups, </cover>for all to relate to. Why did Diego Rivera use symbolism, and what was the goal for his murals? The symbols he chose and the subject matter represent issues of the common people and his murals were a way to get messages and art to the public. What do the two Frida's in the image above represent? The Frida on the left, in the white dress, represents her European identity, and the Frida on the right, in a traditional Mexican peasant dress, represents her Mexican identity.\" , \"start_char\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_1522095625#2_2697536764","rank":6,"relevance":2}]},"time":7},"model_calls":56,"document_judgments":47,"relevance_3":30,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
